/// \defgroup grp_w1018_lan_udp UDP socket wrapper
/// \ingroup grp_network
/// \brief UDP, the class that owns one Winsock datagram socket, and its
///        members: binding, non-blocking reads, socket buffer sizes and
///        broadcast permission.
///
/// UDP sits at the bottom of the game's own network stack (\ref
/// sub_network): Transport, the firewall helper and BFME 2's NAT
/// negotiation code create UDP objects, and everything above them reaches
/// the network through Transport. The class page also covers the
/// constructor and destructor; sending a datagram and the mapping of
/// Winsock errors to status values have no entries here. Start with the
/// class page for the return conventions, which differ between members.
///
/// \see \ref sub_network

/// \class UDP
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief One IPv4 datagram socket: the Winsock wrapper under Transport,
///        the LAN lobby and the firewall helper.
///
/// UDP is the lowest layer of the game's own network stack (\ref
/// sub_network). Each object owns at most one Winsock datagram socket and
/// remembers the local address it was bound to and the last Winsock error
/// it saw. It knows nothing about packets, players or frames: Transport
/// adds the packet format, its checksum and obfuscation on top of it. The
/// lockstep ConnectionManager and the LAN lobby LANAPI talk to Transport
/// rather than to UDP; the firewall helper and BFME 2's NAT negotiation
/// code create UDP objects themselves.
///
/// The class came with Zero Hour, whose udp.cpp says it was taken from the
/// "wnet" library; BFME 2 keeps Zero Hour's interface. Addresses and ports
/// cross the interface in host byte order as plain integers (an
/// UnsignedInt IPv4 address and an UnsignedShort port); UDP converts them
/// to and from network order itself. The exception is the sender address
/// that UDP::Read writes, which is a raw sockaddr_in in network order.
///
/// \par Lifetime and ownership
/// - Construction does not open a socket. BFME 2's constructor marks the
///   socket handle invalid (-1), clears the remembered local address and
///   port, and stores 10000 (Winsock's base error number, WSABASEERR) as
///   the last error. Zero Hour's constructor set the handle to 0 instead.
/// - UDP::Bind(UnsignedInt, UnsignedShort) opens and binds the socket and
///   puts it in non-blocking mode. In BFME 2 an object binds once: a second
///   call while a socket is open fails, and a failed bind closes the socket
///   and marks the handle invalid again, so the same object can be retried.
/// - BFME 2's destructor, when the handle is valid, shuts the socket down
///   in both directions, then calls recvfrom in a loop meant to drain any
///   queued datagrams, then closes it. (Platform behaviour: Winsock rejects
///   receives on a socket shut down for receiving, so the loop ends on its
///   first call and reads nothing.) Zero Hour's destructor only closed a
///   non-zero handle.
/// - Owners allocate UDP objects on the heap and delete them to close the
///   socket. Transport::init creates the socket for Transport's first slot
///   and deletes it again if it cannot be bound. BFME 2's NAT negotiation
///   code (NAT::SetUDPSocketForSlot) binds further sockets with the same
///   one-second retry and installs each one in the Transport slot it is
///   asked for: any of the eight per-player slots, replacing whatever socket
///   that slot held (Zero Hour's Transport had one socket, see \ref
///   sub_network_zh). FirewallHelperClass::openSpareSocket keeps spare
///   sockets for its firewall and NAT detection.
///
/// \par Status codes
/// The members do not share one return convention, which matters to anyone
/// calling them:
/// - Bind, getLocalAddr and SetBlocking return the class's status values:
///   0 (OK) for success, a negative sockStat value for a recognised error.
///   A Winsock error that the class's error table does not list (the
///   address-in-use error is one) comes back from Bind unchanged, as a
///   positive Winsock error number. Callers test for "not 0".
/// - Read returns a byte count, 0 when nothing is waiting, or -1.
/// - SetInputBuffer, SetOutputBuffer and AllowBroadcasts return TRUE (1)
///   on success and FALSE (0) on failure, the opposite sense to Bind.
/// - GetInputBuffer and GetOutputBuffer return a size in bytes.
///
/// \par Modding notes
/// - Nothing in UDP is data-driven: no INI field, map script, Lua call or
///   string table reaches it. Which address and port a socket binds to is
///   decided by its callers; for example BFME 2's LAN lobby
///   (LANAPI::init, LANAPI::SetLocalIP) compiles in its port range.
/// - UDP holds no game state and nothing of it is saved or recorded, so a
///   change here does not by itself affect logic determinism, saves or
///   replays. It can break network play: every machine in a game must
///   still be able to exchange the datagrams that Transport builds.
///
/// \par Remastering notes
/// - This is the Winsock seam of the LAN and lockstep path. The class uses
///   classic Berkeley-style calls through Winsock, including socket, bind,
///   getsockname, recvfrom, sendto, setsockopt, getsockopt, ioctlsocket,
///   shutdown, closesocket, gethostbyname and inet_addr, plus the
///   byte-order helpers htonl and htons. Errors are read with
///   WSAGetLastError; a POSIX port replaces it with errno, closesocket
///   with close and ioctlsocket with fcntl. Transport::init starts Winsock
///   (WSAStartup, version 2.2) before it creates the first socket. A port
///   can map these calls onto POSIX sockets almost one to one; the source
///   still carries the wnet library's POSIX branch for the blocking mode,
///   although the shipped game compiles only the Winsock branch.
/// - It is IPv4-only by interface: addresses are 32-bit integers and the
///   sender address is a sockaddr_in. Supporting IPv6 means changing the
///   interface and every caller that stores addresses, not just this file.
/// - Sockets are non-blocking and polled. There is no select or wait call
///   (Zero Hour's source has its Wait methods commented out), so receive
///   latency is set by how often the frame loop polls Transport.
/// - Outside direct-connect games, BFME 2's LAN lobby sends each message
///   that has no destination to its broadcast address, one copy to each
///   lobby port in its compiled range. These include the game announcements
///   of LANAPI::RequestGameAnnounce and the request sent by
///   LANAPI::RequestLocations. Inside a direct-connect game, messages
///   without a destination go to each other human player instead. A port
///   must keep broadcast working (UDP::AllowBroadcasts), or replace LAN
///   discovery, for game announcements to reach other machines. When the
///   lobby's local address is the loopback address 127.0.0.1,
///   LANAPI::SetLocalIP turns broadcasts off and these messages go to the
///   local address instead.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Interface: Zero Hour GameNetwork/udp.h, which BFME 2's udp.cpp compiles
/// against. The member bodies documented here are Zero Hour's, as carried
/// by the Open-BFME-1 reconstruction and checked against BFME 2's retail
/// bytes; where BFME 2 differs from Zero Hour (Bind's guard and cleanup,
/// the constructor and destructor) the difference is read from the BFME 2
/// bodies. Owners and callers come from BFME 2's retail call graph.
/// \see UDP::Bind, UDP::Read, UDP::AllowBroadcasts, Transport::init,
///      Transport::doRecv, Transport::allowBroadcasts

/// \fn Int UDP::Bind(const char *Host, UnsignedShort port)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Binds the socket to a local address given as text, either a
///        dotted IPv4 address or a host name.
///
/// A convenience overload in front of UDP::Bind(UnsignedInt, UnsignedShort).
/// It turns the text into a host-order address and binds to it. Zero Hour's
/// game code never calls this overload: its Transport resolves names on
/// its own and binds by number. The BFME 2 retail executable contains no
/// direct call to it and no stored pointer to it, so as far as the binary
/// shows, the shipped game never uses it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - If the first character of Host is a digit, the text is parsed as a
///   dotted address with inet_addr. Any other text is looked up with
///   gethostbyname and the first address of the result is used.
/// - Either way the address is converted to host byte order and passed,
///   with port, to the numeric overload, whose result is returned.
/// - Before the lookup, Host is copied into a 100-byte local buffer that
///   is never read again; Zero Hour's body does the same.
///
/// \param Host Address text. Must not be null. A name that starts with a
///             digit is always treated as a dotted address.
/// \param port Local port in host byte order; 0 lets the system choose.
/// \return The numeric overload's status: 0 (OK) on success, otherwise a
///         status value or Winsock error number. When gethostbyname cannot
///         resolve the name the function returns 0, the same value as OK,
///         without creating a socket.
///
/// \warning The unused copy has no length check: a Host string of 100
///          characters or more overruns the local buffer. Only pass short,
///          trusted strings.
/// \see UDP::Bind

/// \fn Int UDP::Bind(UnsignedInt IP, UnsignedShort port)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Opens a datagram socket, binds it to a local address and port,
///        and switches it to non-blocking mode.
///
/// This is how a UDP object comes to life. Transport::init calls it in a
/// loop for up to one second until it succeeds, and gives up (deleting the
/// object) if it never does. BFME 2's NAT negotiation code
/// (NAT::SetUDPSocketForSlot) uses the same one-second retry for the
/// sockets it installs in the Transport slot it is asked for (any of the
/// eight, replacing whatever socket that slot held), and
/// FirewallHelperClass::openSpareSocket binds each spare socket to any
/// local address on the port it was asked for. UDP::Bind(const char *,
/// UnsignedShort) is the only other caller.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. If the object already has an open socket, the call fails at once with
///    UNKNOWN (-1). Zero Hour has no such check: it opened a new socket and
///    lost the old handle.
/// 2. The address and port are stored in network byte order, and a
///    datagram socket (AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM) is created. If that fails the
///    call returns UNKNOWN.
/// 3. The socket is bound. If bind fails, the Winsock error is stored, the
///    status to return is derived from it, and BFME 2 then closes the
///    socket and marks the handle invalid. Zero Hour returned with the
///    failed socket still open, so each retry of Transport::init's loop
///    leaked a socket; in BFME 2 each retry starts from a clean object.
/// 4. On success getsockname reads back the address actually bound (which
///    reveals the port the system chose when port was 0), and the address
///    and port are remembered in host byte order for UDP::getLocalAddr.
/// 5. UDP::SetBlocking puts the socket in non-blocking mode. Its result is
///    ignored; Zero Hour printed a message to stderr when it failed, BFME 2
///    does not check it.
///
/// \param IP   Local IPv4 address in host byte order, 0 for any local
///             interface.
/// \param port Local port in host byte order, 0 to let the system choose.
/// \return 0 (OK) on success. UNKNOWN (-1) if a socket is already open or
///         the socket cannot be created. When bind fails, a negative status
///         value for the Winsock errors the class recognises, otherwise the
///         Winsock error number itself (positive).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The one-second retry in the callers only helps because a failed bind
/// cleans up after itself here; a replacement socket layer should keep
/// that property or change the callers.
/// \see UDP::Bind, UDP::getLocalAddr,
///      UDP::SetBlocking, Transport::init, FirewallHelperClass::openSpareSocket

/// \fn Int UDP::getLocalAddr(UnsignedInt &ip, UnsignedShort &port)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Reports the local address and port the socket was bound to.
///
/// Returns the values UDP::Bind(UnsignedInt, UnsignedShort) remembered
/// after reading them back from the socket, so a port of 0 passed to Bind
/// shows up here as the port the system actually chose. Transport::init,
/// for example, calls it right after a successful bind to record its own
/// local address. The function makes no socket call; before any
/// successful Bind it reports whatever the constructor stored (zeros in
/// BFME 2).
///
/// \param[out] ip   Receives the bound IPv4 address in host byte order. A
///                  socket bound to any interface normally reports 0 here,
///                  not a concrete interface address.
/// \param[out] port Receives the bound port in host byte order.
/// \return Always 0 (OK).
/// \see UDP::Bind, Transport::init

/// \fn Int UDP::Read(unsigned char *msg, UnsignedInt len, sockaddr_in *from)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Receives one waiting datagram without blocking, or reports that
///        none is waiting.
///
/// Because Bind leaves the socket non-blocking, Read never waits, and
/// callers poll it. Transport::doRecv calls it in a loop until it returns
/// 0 or less, draining every occupied socket slot each time it runs.
/// ConnectionManager::update calls doRecv directly, and LANAPI's lobby
/// functions also reach it through Transport. For each datagram, an
/// optional receiver object passed in by doRecv's caller can claim it
/// first. The rest are decrypted and their length and CRC checked, and bad
/// ones are counted and dropped. Each one that passes is copied into the
/// first free entry of Transport's incoming message array (128 entries),
/// which the lockstep connection code and the LAN lobby read. If every
/// entry is occupied, the datagram is discarded without any error, and
/// Transport's statistics count it as received, not as a bad packet.
/// FirewallHelperClass::getManglerResponse reads at most one datagram
/// from each spare socket per call while it waits for a reply to its
/// firewall probe.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// One recvfrom call, with or without a sender address depending on from.
/// Winsock's "would block" error means no datagram is waiting and becomes a
/// return value of 0. Any other error stores the Winsock error in the
/// object and returns -1. A successful read does not clear the stored error.
///
/// \param msg  Destination buffer of at least len bytes.
/// \param len  Size of msg in bytes.
/// \param from Optional; may be null. When given, receives the sender's
///             address as a sockaddr_in in network byte order.
/// \return The size of the datagram in bytes, 0 when nothing is waiting,
///         or -1 on any other error.
///
/// \note Winsock reports a datagram larger than len as an error, not as a
///       truncated read, so such a datagram yields -1. A zero-length
///       datagram also returns 0 and ends a caller's polling loop.
/// \par Remastering
/// POSIX recvfrom truncates an oversized datagram and returns a byte count
/// instead of failing, and reports "would block" as EAGAIN or EWOULDBLOCK.
/// A port that keeps this interface must reproduce the 0 / -1 convention
/// the callers' loops depend on.
/// \see Transport::doRecv, FirewallHelperClass::getManglerResponse, UDP::Bind

/// \fn Int UDP::SetBlocking(Int block)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Switches the socket between blocking and non-blocking mode.
///
/// A private helper. Its only caller is UDP::Bind(UnsignedInt,
/// UnsignedShort), which makes every bound socket non-blocking; the rest of
/// the network code depends on that, because it polls sockets from the
/// frame loop and must never stall waiting for a datagram.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// On Windows a single ioctlsocket call with FIONBIO, passing 1 for
/// non-blocking and 0 for blocking. The source also keeps the original
/// POSIX branch, which sets or clears O_NONBLOCK with fcntl, but the
/// shipped game compiles only the Winsock branch.
///
/// \param block Non-zero for blocking mode, 0 for non-blocking mode.
/// \return 0 (OK) on success, UNKNOWN (-1) if the call fails.
/// \see UDP::Bind, UDP::Read

/// \fn int UDP::GetInputBuffer(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Returns the size of the socket's receive buffer in bytes.
///
/// Reads the system's receive-buffer size for the socket (SO_RCVBUF).
/// Zero Hour's game code never calls it, and the BFME 2 retail executable
/// contains no direct call to it and no stored pointer to it.
///
/// \return The buffer size in bytes, or 0 if getsockopt fails (the error
///         is not reported or stored).
/// \see UDP::SetInputBuffer, UDP::GetOutputBuffer

/// \fn int UDP::GetOutputBuffer(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Returns the size of the socket's send buffer in bytes.
///
/// The send-side twin of UDP::GetInputBuffer: reads SO_SNDBUF for the
/// socket. Like it, it is never called by Zero Hour's game code, and the
/// BFME 2 retail executable neither calls it directly nor stores a pointer
/// to it.
///
/// \return The buffer size in bytes, or 0 if getsockopt fails (the error
///         is not reported or stored).
/// \see UDP::SetOutputBuffer, UDP::GetInputBuffer

/// \fn Int UDP::SetInputBuffer(UnsignedInt bytes)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Asks the system for a receive buffer of the given size.
///
/// Sets SO_RCVBUF on the socket. A larger receive buffer lets more
/// datagrams queue between two polls of UDP::Read before the system starts
/// dropping them. Zero Hour's game code never calls it, and the BFME 2
/// retail executable contains no direct call to it and no stored pointer
/// to it, so the shipped game appears to run with the system's default
/// buffer sizes.
///
/// \param bytes Requested buffer size in bytes, passed to setsockopt as an
///              int. The system may round or cap it; UDP::GetInputBuffer
///              reads back the size actually in effect.
/// \return TRUE (1) if setsockopt succeeded, FALSE (0) otherwise. Note the
///         sense: unlike Bind, non-zero means success.
/// \par Remastering
/// The only knob the class offers for burst tolerance. A port that polls
/// less often than the shipped frame loop, or carries more players, may
/// need larger buffers than the system default. A larger socket buffer does
/// not help once Transport's own incoming array is full: a valid datagram
/// read then is discarded (see UDP::Read).
/// \see UDP::GetInputBuffer, UDP::SetOutputBuffer

/// \fn Int UDP::SetOutputBuffer(UnsignedInt bytes)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Asks the system for a send buffer of the given size.
///
/// The send-side twin of UDP::SetInputBuffer: sets SO_SNDBUF on the
/// socket. Zero Hour's game code never calls it, and the BFME 2 retail
/// executable contains no direct call to it and no stored pointer to it.
///
/// \param bytes Requested buffer size in bytes, passed to setsockopt as an
///              int. The system may round or cap it.
/// \return TRUE (1) if setsockopt succeeded, FALSE (0) otherwise.
/// \see UDP::GetOutputBuffer, UDP::SetInputBuffer

/// \fn Int UDP::AllowBroadcasts(Bool status)
/// \ingroup grp_w1018_lan_udp
/// \brief Allows or forbids sending to a broadcast address from this
///        socket.
///
/// Sets SO_BROADCAST on the socket. Sockets refuse to send to a broadcast
/// address unless this option is on. Zero Hour: LANAPI::sendMessage sends
/// a message with no recipient to the lobby's broadcast address, except in
/// a direct-connect game, where it sends one copy to each other human
/// player. Broadcast is how games on the subnet find each other.
///
/// The only caller in BFME 2's retail executable is
/// Transport::allowBroadcasts, which applies it to the socket in
/// Transport's first slot. BFME 2's LANAPI::init turns broadcasts on after
/// binding. LANAPI::SetLocalIP turns them on too and sets the broadcast
/// address to 255.255.255.255, except when the chosen local address is the
/// loopback address 127.0.0.1: then it turns broadcasts off and uses the
/// local address as the broadcast address. That loopback case is new in
/// BFME 2; Zero Hour's two functions always turned broadcasts on.
///
/// \param status true to allow broadcasts, false to forbid them. It is
///               widened to a Windows BOOL for setsockopt.
/// \return TRUE (1) if setsockopt succeeded, FALSE (0) otherwise.
/// \par Remastering
/// LAN discovery depends on limited broadcast (255.255.255.255) reaching
/// the other machines. Networks and virtual adapters that filter broadcast,
/// and platforms that need an extra permission for it, break the LAN game
/// list. (Zero Hour: direct-connect games address players individually.)
/// \see Transport::allowBroadcasts, LANAPI::init, LANAPI::SetLocalIP
